    scenario:{chapter:3,sourceText:'June put the blue cup down and reached for her coat.',instruction:'Have June tell Nell about the ghost immediately, replacing a solo confrontation with a cooperative investigation.',rewriteText:'June set the cup down exactly where it had been. Outside, she took Nell’s arm.\n\n“I need a warrant. I also need you to listen to something extremely silly without interrupting.”\n\nNell put down her bicycle.\n\n“Ghost?”\n\nJune stared.\n\n“Your grandmother used to complain about them. I was waiting for you to tell me.”',continueText:'They returned with a constable and two evidence bags. Crumb carried a third, announcing that he had been promoted to stationery enforcement.\n\nFor once, June allowed him the last word. She was too relieved not to be doing this alone.',whatIfText:'June trusts Nell with the supernatural clue before Fenn can destroy the cup. The evidence survives, Pip spends less time under suspicion, and their relationship develops through shared work rather than a rescue.',futureText:'On Sunday, Nell arrived before closing with walking shoes and no evidence bags.\n\n“Personal visit,” she said.\n\nCrumb examined her empty hands. “A dramatic improvement.”\n\nJune turned the sign. The last cup had been washed, the water cart had arrived, and for an hour the village could make its own tea.',bridge:'Early disclosure brings a warrant sooner; Fenn cannot remove the cup; Pip is cleared before gossip hardens; June and Nell make plans outside the investigation.',titles:{rewrite:'Tell the sheriff about the ghost',continue_block:'Stationery enforcement',what_if:'What if June asks for help first?',future_jump:'A personal visit',roleplay_session:'Advice from a very unhelpful raven'},delta:{before:'Private suspicion and improvised risk',after:'Shared evidence and mutual trust',description:'June gives Nell a usable lead while accepting that a ghost’s testimony is not enough.'},roleplay:{opening:'Crumb, do you think I am becoming the sort of woman who investigates murders?',reply:'Becoming? You alphabetized the suspects before breakfast. I am concerned that you think this is a recent development.',narration:'Crumb shifts one biscuit carefully behind the sugar bowl without breaking eye contact.',responses:['Tell Nell about the cup. She has a warrant book. You have an apron. Both excellent tools; only one opens a locked surgery legally.','The boy is frightened. Feed him before asking questions. Humans say remarkably less foolish things after bread.','I would like it recorded that I advised caution. In the event of heroism, I shall repeat this at an unbearable volume.']},alternative:{title:'What if June follows the ghost’s accusation alone?',instruction:'Let the supernatural clue be incomplete, so physical evidence and cooperation still matter.',text:'The ghost said the doctor’s name, and June went straight to his door. By the time Nell obtained a warrant, the blue cup was gone. Being right had made her careless; now she had to find proof without the object that could have supplied it.'}},
